<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">King Xie of Zhou</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 750 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> · Also known as Prince Yuchen · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">King Xie of Zhou, personal name Yuchen, was a rival king set up after the fall of the Western Zhou capital in 771 BCE, in opposition to King Ping. Supported by the Duke of Guo and other lords, he ruled for some years at Xie, but around 750 BCE Marquis Wen of Jin killed him, leaving King Ping as the sole Zhou king. He is known mainly from the Bamboo Annals and the Xinian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The rival kingship grew out of a quarrel in the royal house. Around 779 BCE King You of Zhou replaced Queen Shen with his concubine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bao-si">Bao Si</a> and made Bao Si's son Bofu crown prince in place of Ji Yijiu. The queen's father, the Marquess of Shen, joined the Zeng state and a band of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanrong">Quanrong</a> nomads in an attack on the Western Zhou capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haojing">Haojing</a>, in which King You was killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The Marquesses of Shen and Zeng, with Duke Wen of Xu, enthroned Ji Yijiu, the future King Ping, and moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengzhou">Luoyi</a>. At the same time Ji Han, Duke of Guo, conspired with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanrong">Quanrong</a> to raise Ji Yuchen to the throne, and there followed a period of two parallel Zhou kings. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zuo-zhuan">Zuo Zhuan</a> judges harshly: King Xie violated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandate-of-heaven">Mandate of Heaven</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> states usurped his power, and he moved his capital to Jiaru. The stalemate ended when Marquis Wen of Jin killed him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Xinian annals, among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsinghua-bamboo-slips">Tsinghua Bamboo Slips</a> unearthed in recent times, give his posthumous name as King Hui of Xie and record him as a younger brother of King You. The meaning of Xie has been debated. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> scholar Kong Yingda noted that the Bamboo Annals say Han, Duke of Guo, enthroned Yuchen at Xie, suggesting a place, and another scroll of the Annals calls Xie a place of unknown location, though Kong Yingda found other passages that contradict this. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-book-of-tang">New Book of Tang</a> links Xie with Feng, Qi and Li in the ancient Yong Province, which supports the view that it was a place.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “King Xie of Zhou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A rival king, <b>Yuchen</b>, was backed by other lords for some years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-zhou">Western Zhou · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
